Z||000000||falconhulk||08-28-2001||02:07 PM||||I had never heard of this car until yesterday when looking at a automotive buyer's guide.  Hmmmmmm Gm started production on these in 88.  Fiero stopped production in 88.  Styling is very similar.......I wonder if the $20000 dollar price diffrence and the styling similariteshad anything to do with the end of the fiero? Or if the fiero had anything to do with the creation of the Reatta?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroLisa||08-28-2001||02:13 PM||||I have personally never heard of any ties b/w the Fiero and the Reatta... only rumors in terms of other models that killed off the Fiero that I've heard have been the corvette.<BR>Who knows, I like the Reatta tho.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||08-28-2001||02:18 PM||||A friend of mine had a Reatta.  Nice car, but he sold his Grand National to get it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>The Reatta was going to replace the Grand National as their top performance car.  The GN stopped production after 87, when the new 88 Regals went to FWD.  It had a 3800 engine.  Can you imagine how much benefit both cars could have gotten with technology sharing?  The Fiero would have been the hard core sports car and econo coupe and the Reatta would be the gentleman's sports touring car.  That would pretty much guarantee an 89-91 Fiero with the 3800 and 3800SC as soon as they came out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||08-28-2001||03:27 PM||||Believe the Reatta was front engine, front wheel drive, so the drivetrain layout is very different from the Fiero.  Knew a guy that owned one, but I never drove it.  Always liked the looks of the Reatta, and thought it might be collectable... ||||reg||14
Z||000004||HummelHund||08-28-2001||07:28 PM||||The Reatta was basically a two seat Regal with a different body. It even had the Regal dash layout, which included a CRT touchscreen that you controlled the radio and climate control with. It wasn't very fast and was sort of a disappointment when it came to being a sports touring car. They look pretty good, though.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||1
Z||000005||pontiacs4me||08-28-2001||07:38 PM||||I always thought Reattas and fieros shared styling, also.  My dads girlfriend has one, and it is white, as is my fiero, and they were sitting in the driveway beside each other one night, and my dad first made the comment that they looked alot alike.  I looked them over, and they really do.  None of the parts look exactly the same, but if you squint real hard the cars look alot the same.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dlancer407||08-28-2001||08:19 PM||||I have always felt that the big wigs at Buick bitched and moaned to the big wigs at GM that they would have a hard time selling there $ 25,000 to $30,000 dollar two seater. Especially when people could go out and buy a $17,000 dollar Fiero and the car looked better and handled better than the Reatta. I think the same thing happened when Chevy and Oldsmobile wanted to build a convertible. The Cutlass got built but the cheaper Beretta got the boot.||||reg||6
