Z||000011||FieroMaster88||08-28-2001||11:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>I heard they have a small safe hidden behind the seat? Anyone know if that's true?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Not a safe....more of a storage compartment.  We have one of those cars at school in the auto lab.  Not a very good vehicle.  It is not very coumfortable to sit in and seems too roomy.  But hey..whatever makes you happy!<P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000012||kslish||08-28-2001||11:28 PM||||The Reatta was built on a 98.5” shortened wheelbase version of the front wheel drive Riviera chassis.  All the drivetrain and suspension is interchangable between the Reatta and Riv.  Nearly all of the interior was shared with the Riviera also.  This kept the design costs down for GM.<P>Every Reatta was hand crafted in Lansing, MI, at what was then known as the Reatta Craft Centre.  Instead of the usual assembly line, the Reatta was built by small teams who moved the cars being built between "craft stations" on computer guided carts.  It took almost 40 man hours to build a Reatta (forever in modern production terms).  This slow expensive manufacturing process probably ended up killing the car.  Only around 21,000 Reattas (both coupe and convertable) were built during the years the car was in production.  <P>Yes, I do like them     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I was going to buy one off a friend who was upgrading to a Mercedes SLK.  His sister then proceeded to total the car before I could get my hands on it.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Anyone know of a decent one for sale?      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>It's not a sports car by any means, but more like a 2 seat touring coupe.  I'd own one if I could find another decent one at a resonable price.  <P>The only similarity between it and the Fiero that I can think of (besides them both being 2-seaters) is the fact that the Reatta's front fenders are made of a plastic similar to the Fiero's side panels.  The rest of the car is made of sheetmetal. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.reatta.org/BNREATTAS.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A><P><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierospeeder||08-29-2001||01:15 AM||||the reatta was mentioned on the forum, and when i saw it i loved it.<P>try  <A HREF="http://www.reatta.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.reatta.org</A> <P>you can buy one from 1000 bucks and up on ebay or traderonline.<BR>the 88 and 89 had a digital hvac and radio including the dash.<P>the only problem with these cars, is trying to find parts for.   Ya, they lasted for about same as the fiero, but not that many were made<P>The car has abs brakes, and a lot of kewl features.  When it breaks down, its going to cost you.  The engine is the only thing you can find at the junkyard.  And ive read that dealers are having less and less parts for these cars.  ABS is very expensive when it breakes down.  Rotors etc...  <P>I seen only one reatta in my area since i became in love with them.  I will buy one when i get my own place.  Too many cars right now.||||reg||1
